Captured output of image programs writing kitty graphics to a terminal that
answers like havoc does today (ptycap.py, profile havoc-now, or kitty where
noted). The pictures they sent are generated by tests/tools/mkimg.py; no
other data is involved. Versions: chafa 1.14.5, timg 1.6.0, kitty 0.41.1
(kitten icat), mpv from Debian trixie, libpng 1.6.48.

chafa-kitty-grad     chafa -f kitty of grad64x48.png: raw RGBA scaled to
                     66x50, an empty first chunk, 4096-byte chunks padded
                     one by one, an empty last chunk
chafa-kitty-alpha    the same for alpha64x48.png
timg-kitty           timg -p kitty of grad64x48.png: f=100, one chunk
timg-kitty-pattern   timg -p kitty of pattern240x160.png: f=100, chunked
icat-stream          kitten icat --transfer-mode=stream of grad64x48.png
icat-stream-pattern  the same for pattern240x160.png
icat-detect          kitten icat --detect-support: three a=q probes (direct,
                     t=t, t=s) and a DA1 query
icat-kitty           kitten icat of grad64x48.png: the three probes, then
                     t=f (a file path), which havoc refuses
mpv-kitty-shm        mpv --vo=kitty with shared memory: t=s with m=1 on
                     every command; m counts only for direct data, so each
                     command is complete

Not kept: mpv --vo=kitty without shared memory (462 KB). tests/tools/
kitty_ref.py writes an equivalent stream.

regen.sh remakes the files; it needs the tools above and ptycap.py.
